course: dictionary
lesson: 14736
title: Prolongation: making things last longer
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some long words are built from parts you already know. Prolongation comes from the verb prolong, which means to make something last longer. The ending -ation turns the action into a thing, a noun, so a prolongation is the making of something longer.
explain: Read this: «The meeting ran late, so the prolongation of the meeting took an extra hour.» The meeting did not stop; it went on for longer. The clues are the word longer and the time mentioned, an extra hour.
explain: Prolongation can also be about length in space. «The maths teacher drew a prolongation of the line, past the end of the paper.» The line is made longer in distance, not time. It is the same idea, a different kind of length.
explain: When you meet prolongation, look for the short word long inside it and ask: is something being made longer? Then try swapping in extension and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Prolongation is a noun: one prolongation, several prolongations.
fact: Prolongation means making something longer in time, such as a meeting or a stay.
fact: Prolongation can also mean making something longer in space, such as a line.
fact: Prolongation comes from the verb prolong, which means to make something last longer.
fact: Extension is a more common everyday word with a similar meaning, so you will often hear it in ordinary talk.
exercise: «The meeting had a prolongation, so it went on for an extra hour.» What does prolongation mean here? | Moving the meeting to another room | Cancelling the meeting | Making something smaller | *Making something longer in time
exercise: «The maths teacher drew a prolongation of the line.» What is being made longer? | The paper | The pencil | *The line | The teacher
exercise: Which everyday word is closest in meaning to prolongation? | Silence | Shortcut | *Extension | Breakfast
test: «The prolongation of our holiday made us very happy.» What is being made longer? | *Our holiday | Our happy faces | Our house | Our homework
test: Which sentence uses prolongation correctly? | *The doctor spoke about the prolongation of the patient's life | I will prolongation the bus tomorrow | The prolongation is bright red | The prolongation ate my lunch
exam: Which of these is a noun? | Long | Prolong | *Prolongation | Longer
